# 11. POLICY ACTIVATION AND IMMEDIATE ENFORCEMENT

Policy changes must become effective immediately after successful save.

Examples:

- An IP is removed.
- A GPS location is changed.
- A Group is disabled.
- A Role's policy changes.
- A Role's assigned Groups change.
- An AND/OR condition changes.
- A temporary approval expires.

All these changes must trigger immediate policy revalidation.

### Existing Active Sessions

Do not validate connectivity only at login.

Protected API requests must also enforce the current policy.

When a previously authorized user becomes unauthorized:

- Reject the next protected API request.
- Terminate or invalidate the unauthorized session according to the existing authentication architecture.
- Clear access to protected application data.
- Redirect the user to the Access Restricted screen.
- Do not allow cached frontend data to bypass server authorization.

Use reliable policy versioning or invalidation mechanisms.

Do not depend only on an in-memory cache.

All backend instances must observe policy updates.

Super Admin must remain unrestricted by connectivity policy.

